## Feature Engineering

**`data_manager.py`** performs all preprocessing. New columns added:

| Feature | How calculated | Why |
|---------|---------------|-----|
| `year` | Extracted from Month_Beginning | Captures long-term trend |
| `month_num` | 1–12 | Raw month number |
| `month_sin`, `month_cos` | sin/cos of (2π × month/12) | Encodes cyclical seasonality — December and January are close, not far apart |
| `quarter` | 1–4 | Quarterly seasonality |
| `demand_lag_1` | MonthTotal from 1 month ago | Last month's demand predicts this month's |
| `demand_lag_3` | MonthTotal from 3 months ago | Quarterly memory |
| `demand_lag_12` | MonthTotal from 12 months ago | Same month last year — strongest predictor |
| `demand_rolling_3` | 3-month rolling average | Smoothed recent trend |
| `demand_rolling_12` | 12-month rolling average | Long-term baseline |
| `demand_growth_rate` | % change vs 12 months ago | YoY momentum |
| `demand_category` | low/medium/high based on percentiles | Categorical demand level |

**10 features used in model training** (FEATURE_COLS): `year`, `month_num`, `month_sin`, `month_cos`, `quarter`, `demand_lag_1`, `demand_lag_3`, `demand_lag_12`, `demand_rolling_3`, `demand_rolling_12`

**Why sin/cos encoding for months:** If you encode months as 1–12, the model thinks December (12) and January (1) are far apart numerically. But they're adjacent seasonally. Sin/cos encoding wraps them so December and January are close in feature space.

